Sources:
- earthlyskeptic, eleven Theodolite screenshots (Google Drive folder
  1xbZ5I3DVk3bWS3jWXljhcXMoKafTnBjE), the table of his readings (Desmos graph) and the document
  naming the two flights (Flights, Model, Graphs.docx)
  <https://www.desmos.com/calculator/bkxuurlmlh>
- FlightAware, track logs of AA 6322 (5 September 2025, KORD-KDSM) and AA 5255 (8 September 2025,
  KDSM-KDCA), ADS-B
  <https://www.flightaware.com/live/flight/AAL6322/history/20250905/1210Z/KORD/KDSM/tracklog>
- Open-Meteo historical forecast API, NOAA GFS (gfs_seamless), pressure levels 1000-100 hPa
  <https://open-meteo.com/en/docs/historical-forecast-api>
- Copernicus DEM GLO-90, European Space Agency <https://doi.org/10.5270/ESA-c5d3d65>
Evidence class (rule 10): check
Where the Earth's radius enters:
- FD_POINTS[].h, FD_POINTS[].zh, FD_POINTS[].zhA, FD_POINTS[].tilt: the heights come from the
  transponders' pressure altitudes placed in the GFS model's air (geopotential heights), the terrain
  at the horizon is found along the heading on a 6,371 km sphere, and the accelerations come from
  ADS-B ground speeds (GNSS)
What would vindicate the flat model (rule 5): Dips that stay near zero at every height, or that grow
  linearly with height (a far edge at a fixed distance), would favour the plane; dips growing as the
  square root of the height above the horizon terrain, with one radius for all, favour the globe.
